WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.490
MINHAZ KAZI: Data Studio
is Google's next generation

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.640
business intelligence and data
visualization platform that

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.650
lets you build
scalable serverless

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:11.450
interactive dashboards on
the cloud free of charge.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:12.180
Hi.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.440
I'm Minhaz Kazi, developer
advocate for Data Studio.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.380
In this video, we'll take a look
at two new exciting features

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.530
in Data Studio--

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.522
Explorer and Data Blending.

00:00:21.522 --> 00:00:22.980
I'll also show you
how you can take

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.530
advantage of these
features in Data Studio.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.360
When you're working
with a data set,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.400
you want to first
explore the data.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.770
You want to start with
an overview of the data,

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.290
explore it, and
look for insights.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.230
To quote Ben Schneiderman's
visualization mantra,

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:41.320
overview first, zoom and
filter, and details on demand.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.580
So when you're doing more of
an exploratory visualization,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.070
you need the capability
to select exactly the data

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.810
you need, [INAUDIBLE] based
on different criteria,

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:53.520
and try different visualizations
to see what makes more sense.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.490
Data Studio Explorer is
a dedicated space for you

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.150
to quickly derive
insights from your data

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.890
through fast ad-hoc querying.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.950
It is built on top of the
same stack as Data Studio.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.390
So everything
created in Explorer

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.820
can be exported into
Data Studio reporting.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.430
And everything visualized
in Data Studio reporting

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.650
can be exported into Data
Explorer for quick analysis.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.460
This Explorer feature
becomes more important when

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.060
you're working with big data.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.000
For example, in BigQuery.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.560
BigQuery is Google's petabyte
scale data warehouse solution.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.917
In BigQuery you can use SQL
to query terabytes in seconds.

00:01:28.917 --> 00:01:31.250
Let's see how you can use
Data Studio's Explorer feature

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.270
with a BigQuery data set.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.340
You're going to love this.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.449
Once you have your data in
BigQuery, what can you do?

00:01:38.449 --> 00:01:39.990
What if you want to
quickly visualize

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.580
the results of your data?

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.250
Or what if you have users who
don't know how to write SQL,

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.270
but still need to
work with the data?

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:47.550
So here's BigQuery.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.590
And we'll pick a public
dataset from here.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.720
This is the public data set
for Major League Baseball.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.330
And we'll pick this table.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.170
And we will run a sample query.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.250
We'll pick all the fields.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.850
And we'll remove the limit.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.949
And then we'll run the query.

00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:13.176
So once the query
is run, then we

00:02:13.176 --> 00:02:14.550
can see the results
of the query.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:18.032
We also have this button
called Exploring Data Studio.

00:02:18.032 --> 00:02:19.740
If we click this
button, what will happen

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.110
is that Data Studio will
create a direct connection

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.490
to the BigQuery table.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.190
And we'll set up the adhoc
visual querying interface

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.790
where I can drag and drop
chips that generate the SQL

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.340
queries in BigQuery.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.020
So this table contains every
Major League Baseball event

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.660
at bat from 2016.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.090
So first, let's say
we want to answer

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.940
the question, what is the
venue capacity of each venue?

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.470
For that, first we'll
look for the venue cities.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.230
We'll add the city to dimension.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.390
And we'll add the
capacity to the matrix.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.992
And we can see that
these capacities

00:02:58.992 --> 00:03:00.450
are very large
numbers because it's

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.940
summing up all the results.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:06.610
So instead of the sum,
we'll pick average.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:09.740
And now we can see the venue
capacity of these venues.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:14.150
So let's say we want to
also filter this by state.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:18.200
So we'll pick the states,
and we'll add the venue state

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.900
measure as a filter.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:26.220
And select California
as the filtering state.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.390
And we'll apply.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:30.830
And now we can
see all the venues

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.450
in California and the
relevant capacities.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.170
This is how easy it is to
use this Explorer interface.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:41.390
However, I can also switch
this table to a bar chart.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.475
Or I can even switch
this to a geomap.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:47.950
I can also remove this filter.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.714
And then let's say,
whatever results I have,

00:03:53.714 --> 00:03:55.380
I want to put this
in a report and share

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.210
that with my stakeholders.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:58.560
That is also possible to do.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.520
I will save this
Explorer option.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.500
And then I will
take this and put it

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.000
into a new Data Studio report.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:15.240
Data Explorer is 100%
interoperable with Data Studio

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:15.760
reporting.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.940
So I can move
visualizations created here

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.589
to Data Studio reporting
with one click.

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:23.170
And now Data Studio is
exporting my visualizations

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:24.910
to this report.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:27.370
And here you can see the report.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:31.570
I can now add a
filter for the state.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:35.980
And then I can share this report
with my other stakeholders

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:39.980
where they can easily
filter and see the results.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.220
If you want to get valuable
insights from your data,

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.650
you might bring in data
from different sources.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:47.290
Then you want to combine,
analyze, and visualize

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:49.750
your combined data as a whole.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:51.340
When you combine
multiple data sets

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:54.490
based on common parameters,
the visualization

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.690
gives you better
context to the data.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.960
For example, you might want
to join your daily sales

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.630
data by geographic
region with that region's

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:04.500
daily average temperature to
see how the temperature affected

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:05.140
sales.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.510
This can be useful if
you are, let's say,

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:08.950
an ice cream manufacturer.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.560
Data Studio lets you bring in
data from different sources

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.140
and visualize them side by side
on the same dashboard page.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.870
However, the Data
Blending feature

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:18.580
will let you join
those data sets,

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:21.580
and analyze, filter,
and visualize them

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:23.770
in the same visual component.

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:28.300
Let's see how easily Data
Blending works in Data Studio.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.490
In this example, we'll
walk through how easy

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.580
it is to blend your
data in Data Studio.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.440
We'll blend your
Google Analytics data

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.224
with some public data.

00:05:37.224 --> 00:05:38.890
If you want to do
this without blending,

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:41.320
it will require exporting
data from the original system,

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:45.040
like Analytics, using an
API or manual exports,

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.560
and then joining that data
in a tool like Google Sheets

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:48.940
or BigQuery.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.850
Using blending, you can
combine the data in Data Studio

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.370
and continue to take advantage
of the direct connection

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:55.930
to your data.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:59.160
To get started, let's assume
you have two data sources.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.910
The first one is the
Google Analytics data

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.310
and a set of public data
sets that shows the US

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.770
population on a state basis.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.680
So let's say we'll add the
public information first.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:17.980
We will add the population
public data source.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:20.140
For dimension, we
will add region.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:24.860
And for metric, we will
add the population.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.900
So you can see each region
and their population.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.450
So we'll add a
second table where

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.340
we'll bring in the
Google Analytics data.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:45.620
And similarly, over here, we'll
bring in region and also user

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:46.120
count.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.460
So what we want to do is we want
to blend these two data sources

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.050
and have one table
where we'll have

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.140
the region, the
population, and the users.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.430
And it is very easy to
do that in Data Studio.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:06.630
What we will do is we'll
select both of the tables.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:07.890
Right click.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.710
Select Blend Data.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:12.270
And then Data
Studio will give us

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:15.810
a new table that will have a new
blended data source with both

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.750
of these fields present.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:22.770
So as you can see over here
in this table that is blended,

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:25.850
we have the region, the
population, and the users.

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:29.610
And that is how easy it is
to blend data in Data Studio.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:32.430
So Data Explorer lets you
quickly generate insights

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:34.680
from your data, in
addition to leveraging

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.870
powerful visualizations
in reports.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.030
And Data Blending lets
you join your data

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.370
from multiple sources,
and visualize them

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:44.127
in the same component to
provide additional context.

00:07:44.127 --> 00:07:46.710
These new features will help you
to get a better understanding

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.710
of your data and get actionable
insights in a shorter

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.060
time in Data Studio.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:54.210
Go to datastudio.google.com
and try out these new features

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:55.100
today.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

